            {
                "title": "A mobile esports final in Lahore peaked at 1.14 million while a Thai final's peak rose 7.6 percent and its hours watched fell nearly 18 percent, and a World Series game drew a 12.26 million average on American television",
                "hook": "Three events, three metrics, and at one of them the watch time fell while the peak rose.  Also, a wrong viewing figure for the World Series went viral because somebody screenshotted an AI answer.",
                "open": True,
                "stamps": [
                    ("ESPORTS CHARTS · PMGO · 27 OCT", "https://escharts.com/news/pmgo-2026-season-2-viewership"),
                    ("ESPORTS CHARTS · ROV · 28 OCT", "https://escharts.com/news/rov-pro-league-2026-winters-viewership"),
                    ("SPORTS MEDIA WATCH · 28 OCT", "https://www.sportsmediawatch.com/2026/10/world-series-viewership-brewers-rays-opens-not-far-off-dodgers-jays/"),
                    ("YAHOO SPORTS · FAKE FIGURE · 24 OCT", "https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/false-reports-2026-world-series-212334716.html"),
                ],
                "body": [
                    "The PUBG Mobile Global Open second season finished in Lahore on <mark>25 October</mark> with 32 teams and a 500,000 dollar prize pool.  Esports Charts puts the <mark>peak at 1.14 million concurrent viewers</mark> — people watching at the same moment — <mark>up 6.09 percent on the record set earlier this year</mark>, with <mark>8.7 million hours watched</mark>.  No average concurrent figure and no total airtime were published, which means the average cannot be calculated — and the average is the number a sponsor's logo was actually in front of.",
                    "Two structural details from that event are worth more than the peak.  <mark>YouTube's share of cumulative watch time fell below 50 percent</mark>, with Kick and TikTok taking the difference.  And <mark>co-casting watch time rose about ten points to roughly half the total</mark>, with unique broadcasting channels going from <mark>336 to 455</mark>.  Co-casting means a creator restreaming the official feed with their own commentary over it.  Half the watch time went to somebody else's version of the broadcast you sponsored.",
                    "Then the counter-example, which makes the house rule in one line.  The Arena of Valor professional league in Thailand finished on the same day.  Its <mark>peak rose 7.6 percent on the previous split, to 221,490</mark>.  Its <mark>hours watched fell 17.78 percent</mark>.  Same event, two numbers, opposite directions — and Esports Charts attributes the fall to fewer channels carrying it, with <mark>official channels taking nearly 89 percent of watch time</mark> as community casters stepped back.  If you had bought that sponsorship on hours watched you would have been punished for other people not restreaming it.",
                    "For scale against something recognisable: the World Series opened in Milwaukee with an <mark>average audience of 12.26 million for Game 1 and 10.85 million for Game 2</mark>, down about <mark>8 and 7 percent</mark> on last year's series.  That is a Nielsen average audience across a broadcast network and several Spanish-language and streaming feeds, which is a different measure from a concurrent viewer count, so compare them with care.  The honest version: the Lahore peak is roughly <mark>9 percent of the World Series Game 1 average</mark>.",
                    "One more thing from the same week, and it is a free lesson.  A figure of <mark>15.2 million viewers for World Series Game 1 went viral before Nielsen had published anything</mark>, from a screenshot of an AI-generated answer.  <mark>15.2 million was the 2024 Game 1 figure</mark>.  Somebody asked a chatbot, it answered confidently with an old number, and the screenshot travelled faster than the measurement.",
                ],
                "flagnote": "Both esports figures come from Esports Charts alone.  The 1.14 million peak is published rounded with no exact figure, and that event's average concurrents, total airtime, start date and sponsors are all unpublished, and the platform shift is given as a direction past the 50 percent line rather than as exact shares.  The Arena of Valor item gives no absolute hours-watched figure, only the percentage change; Esports Charts states that Chinese platforms are excluded from its tracking, so both peaks are lower than the true global figures.  Neither article names a brand sponsor, so this item makes no claim about what any sponsor bought.  The World Series figures are Nielsen's current big-data-plus-panel methodology, which is not comparable with pre-2024 series, and the two sources differ on the year-on-year framing — one gives 8 and 7 percent per game, a wire report describes it as down 6 percent through two games, and the arithmetic on the published averages gives 7.3 percent across both.  No peak audience is published for either baseball game, and it is not stated whether streaming is folded into the average.  Game 3 was played on 26 October and its rating has not been published.  The comparison between a concurrent peak and a Nielsen average is this brief's arithmetic and the two metrics are not equivalent.",
                "so_what": "Hours watched is a product of how many people watched, for how long, across how many channels — so it rises when an event adds restreamers and falls when they lose interest, even when the audience at the peak is growing.  Thailand's final proves it from both ends in one week: more people at the peak, nearly a fifth less watch time, because the community stopped carrying it.  Meanwhile half the watch time at the Lahore final went to a creator's version rather than the official feed, which is where your logo was not.  And the fake World Series number is the same failure in miniature — a confident figure with no source behind it moved further than the real one.",
                "do_this": "Write average concurrent viewers into every live sponsorship as the contracted measure, with peak reported alongside and hours watched excluded, and ask for total airtime in the same breath so you can divide it yourself when a seller only offers you hours.  Then ask specifically what share of watch time came from co-casting channels rather than the official feed, and whether your branding appeared on those feeds at all — on a mobile esports final that is now roughly half the watch time.  And before you repeat any viewing figure this week, click through to the measurement firm that published it.",
            },
