            {
                "title": "Glease Man put the cost of the breakdown in the title instead of the name of the job, and his median views went from 20,864 to 319,009",
                "hook": "282,000 subscribers.  A Canadian machinist fixing parts the manufacturer will not sell you.  No sponsor, no discount code and no affiliate link in any description across the entire relaunch — though the videos themselves were not watched.",
                "open": True,
                "stamps": [
                    ("CHANNEL", "https://www.youtube.com/@gleaseman"),
                    ("TOP VIDEO", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_cQZxjxPH9E"),
                ],
                "body": [
                    "Glease Man is John, who runs Glease Manufacturing, a jobbing machine shop in Southern Ontario that takes one-off repair work from whoever walks in.  <mark>282,000 subscribers</mark> as YouTube displays it, 103 videos, <mark>94,050,980 lifetime views</mark>, on the platform since 2014.  What he films is heavy-equipment repair that the original manufacturer either will not sell parts for or cannot deliver in time — hydraulic cylinder re-barrelling, which means fitting a new outer tube to a cylinder instead of buying a whole new one, cracked cast-iron hubs, and a water-jacketed Caterpillar exhaust manifold.",
                    "The numbers.  Across the <mark>eight long-form uploads since January, median views were 319,009</mark> — median meaning the middle video, so one hit cannot carry the figure.  Across the <mark>twelve before them, median 20,864</mark>.  That is <mark>15.3 times</mark>.  And it is not one video doing the work: remove his biggest, the 1,685,573, and the median is <mark>310,983</mark>.  Remove the two biggest and it is still <mark>259,112</mark>, a <mark>12.4 times</mark> step-up.  Five separate uploads in the recent block sit between 207,000 and 1.69 million against an old median of twenty thousand.",
                    "Be precise about the weak spot, because there is one.  The two blocks <mark>do overlap</mark> — his worst recent video at 69,904 is beaten by four of the twelve older ones, including a 464,717 tutorial.  So this is a very strong median case rather than a clean no-overlap case.  It is still a channel that changed category.",
                    "The pivot has a date: <mark>7 January 2026</mark>, the first upload after thirteen months of silence.  Five things moved at once, and one of them is doing most of the work.  <mark>The titles stopped naming the job and started naming the money</mark>: This part costs 10,000 dollars PER DAY until FIXED, It only costs 100K to replace this part, There's NO PARTS available for this 1,000,000 dollar machine, There's a 2500 dollar difference between these two cylinders.  Compare the old regime — a shop press build, something called a Poop Rocket.",
                    "The other four, in order of how much they matter.  Subject moved from his own projects and how-to-make-money tutorials to <mark>customer breakdown jobs</mark>, so every video has a stranger's deadline in it.  Runtime went <mark>up</mark>, hard — the three biggest recent uploads run <mark>47 to 49 minutes</mark> against a pre-hiatus norm of eight to thirty.  Cadence went the other way: the old every-other-Friday promise is gone from every description and he is now roughly monthly, publishing when a job walks in.  And the format is now a case study — machine is down, here is the repair, here is what it saved.",
                    "Now the commercial picture, which is why this is a phone call.  <mark>Seven of the last ten descriptions carry no commercial link of any kind</mark>.  <mark>Zero named sponsors across the channel.  Zero discount codes.  Zero affiliate links</mark> — the only two product links anywhere are share links to a book about Crohn's disease with no affiliate tag on them.  He has even <mark>dropped his own tip jar</mark>, which appears in the pre-hiatus videos and in none of the eight since.",
                    "And here is what is on camera, unpaid.  A large engine lathe run with a steady rest because the cylinder rods are too long to pass through the spindle.  Cylinder boring and honing.  TIG welding, the slow hand-fed kind, on cast iron and on a water-jacketed cast exhaust manifold, which is about as difficult as welding on camera gets.  <mark>No tooling brand, no welding brand, no consumables brand, no hydraulics brand is named anywhere</mark>.  The only badged iron in shot belongs to his customers — Caterpillar, John Deere, New Holland.",
                ],
                "watch": {
                    "label": "TOP VIDEO, LAST 3 MONTHS",
                    "title": "There's NO PARTS available for this $1,000,000 machine",
                    "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_cQZxjxPH9E",
                    "meta": "1,685,573 views · published 6 September 2026 · 47m 35s",
                    "note": "A forty-seven-minute single-job film in which a part for a million-dollar machine does not exist to buy, so he makes one, and the whole retention structure is the viewer waiting to see whether it works.",
                },
                "flagnote": "Per-video view counts, dates and runtimes are exact figures pulled from YouTube today and counters move, so treat them as a snapshot.  Medians exclude Shorts, and one 76-second item was removed on that basis.  Older view counts are lifetime totals rather than velocity, so some of the gap is age-related accumulation — though the recent block is also the newer one, which makes the step-up conservative rather than inflated.  The subscriber figure is YouTube's abbreviated count, so 282,000 is rounded.  The block overlap is stated plainly in the body and is a real weakness in the evidence.  One check could not be completed, and it is the important one: the sponsor inventory is built from video descriptions only and the videos were not watched, so a placement read aloud on camera, shown in a lower third, or flagged by YouTube's own paid-promotion banner would not appear in that list.  Given how much high-value tooling is in frame, ask before anyone builds a pitch on the nobody-is-paying-him line.  His Shorts descriptions and his other social accounts were not checked.  The split of those 94 million lifetime views between Shorts and long-form is unknown.  No agency or management is named anywhere in the descriptions, but absence cannot be proven.  He has not published a long-form video since 6 September, so the cadence is a genuine risk.  The featured video was confirmed against YouTube's own public title-lookup endpoint, which returned a matching title and author name.",
                "so_what": "He did not change what he films.  He changed what the title is about, from the task to the consequence of not doing the task, and the median held at twelve times the old one even after you throw out his two biggest videos.  That is the lesson worth carrying out of this issue: a video about fixing a hydraulic cylinder is a hobby video, and a video about a machine costing somebody ten thousand dollars a day while it sits there is a business story that anyone who has ever had equipment break will watch for forty-seven minutes.  The commercial consequence is that he has accidentally built the perfect environment for anyone selling into downtime — the product is the only thing between a stranger and a number he has already put in the title.",
                "do_this": "Take him to a hydraulic cylinder component supplier, a cutting-tool or insert brand, a welding consumables maker, or anyone selling portable line-boring and honing equipment into mine, farm, quarry and municipal fleet shops.  Sell integration inside one forty-five-minute job film rather than a sponsor read, and pitch on downtime cost and parts availability rather than on product features, because that is the argument he is already making for free.  Go this week while he is between uploads and price against the 319,009 median rather than the 282,000 subscribers, and before you promise exclusivity, ask him directly whether anything in the shop was given, lent or discounted.",
            },
